1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:01,200
It's midnight.

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,420
You went to bed four hours ago.

3
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:05,090
And now you're wide awake.

4
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:08,189
And so is everyone else in your house.

5
00:00:08,189 --> 00:00:12,000
Because for most of history, people didn't sleep through the night.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,200
They slept twice.

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,820
Welcome to night in medieval England.

8
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:22,220
Up to sixteen hours of darkness, no light switch, and a whole list of things out there that want you.

9
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,670
That meter in the corner?

10
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:25,440
Your odds of seeing sunrise.

11
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:31,600
And stick around, because one man's midnight habit got so bad, his own family moved out.

12
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:33,230
Let's start at sunset.

13
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:36,280
In winter, that's before four in the afternoon.

14
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:39,070
And for you, the day is basically over.

15
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:42,030
Light costs money, and you don't have money.

16
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:43,660
So you do what everyone does.

17
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:46,180
You eat, you pray, and you go to bed.

18
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:46,790
Early.

19
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:47,890
Really early.

20
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:50,580
You already know the bed situation.

21
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:54,170
Straw, wool blanket, the whole family.

22
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:54,680
Cozy.

23
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,680
And here's a detail you didn't expect.

24
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:03,880
Medical books, from the late Middle Ages onward, told you exactly how to lie: on your right side for your first sleep,

25
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,180
then turn to your left.

26
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,300
It was supposed to help digestion.

27
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:10,040
Even your sleeping position had instructions.

28
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,400
So you fall asleep.

29
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,160
And if you wake up feeling like something heavy is sitting on your chest,

30
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:16,910
well, you have a word for that.

31
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:17,830
The nightmare.

32
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:20,760
And in the Middle Ages, it wasn't a bad dream.

33
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:25,250
The "mare" was a creature, a spirit that sat on sleepers in the night.

34
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:30,100
The word shows up in English around the year 1300, describing exactly that.

35
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,680
And the mare wasn't the only thing out there.

36
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:40,200
Around 1400, a monk at Byland Abbey, in Yorkshire, filled the blank pages of a manuscript with local ghost stories.

37
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,600
And these weren't ghosts that went "boo".

38
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,120
They were souls stuck in purgatory, and they wanted something.

39
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:47,470
Usually, prayers.

40
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:51,000
Sometimes, they wouldn't leave you alone until they got them.

41
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,910
So people protected their homes.

42
00:01:52,910 --> 00:02:01,460
In Chaucer's Canterbury Tales, written in the late 1300s, a carpenter says a "night-spell" at the four corners of his house and at the door.

43
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:03,700
Basically, medieval home security.

44
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:05,720
And now, it's midnight.

45
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,570
And you wake up.

46
00:02:06,570 --> 00:02:07,940
Not because of a noise.

47
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,630
Not because of the ghost.

48
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,640
Just because it's time.

49
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:17,070
Historian Roger Ekirch spent years digging through old diaries, court records and books,

50
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:22,940
and found hundreds, and eventually thousands, of references to a "first sleep" and a "second sleep".

51
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,610
In France, they called it "premier somme".

52
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:27,120
In Italy, "primo sonno".

53
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:29,040
Even Chaucer mentions it.

54
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,780
In one of his tales, a princess goes to bed just after dark,

55
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:36,000
sleeps her "first sleep", and wakes up.

56
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,620
So between your two sleeps, you've got about an hour.

57
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:40,120
Maybe more.

58
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,080
In the dark.

59
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:43,480
What do you do with it?

60
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:45,530
Option one: you pray.

61
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:48,120
This was considered a perfect time for it.

62
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:51,570
Quiet, dark, nobody asking you for anything.

63
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:53,120
Monks took it seriously.

64
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:57,960
The Rule of Saint Benedict told them to get up at the eighth hour of the night in winter,

65
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,560
around two in the morning, for the night prayers.

66
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:01,480
Every night.

67
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:04,820
Option two: you think about your dreams.

68
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,350
Because to you, dreams meant something.

69
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:11,550
The most popular dream book of the Middle Ages, the "Dream Book of Daniel",

70
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:17,240
was basically an alphabetical list of things you might dream about, and whether they meant good luck or bad.

71
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,790
Option three: chores.

72
00:03:19,790 --> 00:03:20,710
Check the fire.

73
00:03:20,710 --> 00:03:21,880
Check the animals.

74
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,440
Use the chamber pot, which lived under the bed.

75
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:26,750
Yes, in the same room.

76
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:29,800
If you had a baby, this was feeding time.

77
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:34,600
A French farce from the 1400s shows parents getting up at midnight to rock,

78
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:36,450
walk and feed their child.

79
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:37,960
Some things never change.

80
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:38,830
Option four.

81
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:45,720
A French doctor in the 1500s, Laurent Joubert, wrote that working people had their best,

82
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:51,330
ahem, "couple time" after the first sleep, because they weren't exhausted anymore.

83
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:52,740
Thank you, doctor.

84
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:54,200
Very helpful.

85
00:03:54,200 --> 00:04:00,360
And option five: according to Ekirch's research, some people even got up and visited the neighbors.

86
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:01,960
In the middle of the night.

87
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:07,080
Imagine your neighbor knocking at one in the morning, and that's normal.

88
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:08,440
But here's the thing.

89
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:10,500
You probably shouldn't go far.

90
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:14,120
Because in medieval England, the night had its own rules.

91
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:21,240
The Statute of Winchester, in 1285, ordered the gates of walled towns closed from sunset to sunrise,

92
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:25,130
and set a watch: in a city, six men at every gate,

93
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:26,620
all night long.

94
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:29,220
And if you were a stranger walking around at night?

95
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:31,240
The watch could hold you until morning.

96
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:35,220
If they still didn't like you then, they handed you over to the sheriff.

97
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:38,920
And even without the law, the dark was dangerous on its own.

98
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:43,100
No streetlights, unpaved roads, ditches, rivers.

99
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:47,610
Getting lost on your way home was a real way to end your night.

100
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:48,810
Permanently.

101
00:04:48,810 --> 00:04:51,160
Now, remember the man whose family moved out?

102
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:53,080
Here he is.

103
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:56,330
His name was Pierre de Béarn, a French nobleman.

104
00:04:56,330 --> 00:05:00,760
The chronicler Jean Froissart wrote that after he killed a huge bear,

105
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:05,640
Pierre started getting up in his sleep and grabbing his sword, fighting nobody.

106
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:08,890
And when he couldn't find it, he made such a racket that,

107
00:05:08,890 --> 00:05:14,470
in Froissart's words, it seemed like all the demons of hell were in there with him.

108
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:16,920
His family eventually left.

109
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,020
Honestly?

110
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:18,980
Fair.

111
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:19,560
Anyway.

112
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:24,510
After an hour or so, you get sleepy again, and you go back for your second sleep,

113
00:05:24,510 --> 00:05:26,020
until the sun comes up.

114
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:27,800
And here's the crazy part.

115
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:35,960
In the early 1990s, a psychiatrist named Thomas Wehr put volunteers in darkness for fourteen hours a day,

116
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:36,900
for a month.

117
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:38,940
No lamps, no screens.

118
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:43,400
By the fourth week, their sleep had split in two all by itself.

119
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:48,100
About four hours of sleep, one or two hours awake, then four more hours.

120
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,280
Just like the old records described.

121
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:55,000
So if you sometimes wake up at three in the morning and can't sleep,

122
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,050
maybe nothing's wrong with you.

123
00:05:57,050 --> 00:05:59,490
Maybe your body is just a little medieval.

124
00:05:59,490 --> 00:06:02,570
To be fair, not every historian agrees.

125
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:07,080
Some think "first sleep" could just mean the first part of one sleep,

126
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:08,650
and that the waking was short.

127
00:06:08,650 --> 00:06:10,840
But the experiment is hard to ignore.

128
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:14,440
And then, finally, the sun comes up.

129
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:20,760
You survived the nightmare, the ghosts, the watch, the ditches and a neighbor knocking at one in the morning.

130
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:25,220
And now you get to work in the fields for the next ten hours.

131
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:27,700
And tonight, you do it all again.

132
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:28,670
So, be honest.

133
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:31,460
You wake up at midnight with an hour to kill.

134
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:32,280
What are you doing?

135
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:35,340
Praying, chores, or visiting the neighbors?

136
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:36,600
Tell me in the comments.

137
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:41,020
And if you want to know what else medieval peasants did when nobody was watching,

138
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:42,120
you know what to do.
